<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madiun Affair</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1948</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Madiun uprising · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-national-revolution">Indonesian National Revolution</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madiun">Madiun</a> Affair was a communist uprising in September 1948 centred on the town of Madiun in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>, during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>'s war of independence against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. Leftist troops and supporters of the Communist Party, whose leader Musso had recently returned from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, seized the town and proclaimed a new government. The Republican army crushed the revolt within weeks and Musso was killed, leaving lasting hostility between the army and the communists.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madiun-affair.jpg" alt="Madiun Affair" width="320" height="234" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nationaal Archief · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Groep_IIndonesische_mannen_wordt_met_touwen_geboeid_en_onder_bewaking_afgevoerd_,_Bestanddeelnr_8806.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
